mplate="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8256x4644+0+430/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fb7%2F32%2F0a4ebbfe4f2dbabf8c9657e981f6%2Fgettyimages-2297178413.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8256x4644+0+430/resize/1100/quality/50/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fb7%2F32%2F0a4ebbfe4f2dbabf8c9657e981f6%2Fgettyimages-2297178413.jpg" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8256x4644+0+430/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fb7%2F32%2F0a4ebbfe4f2dbabf8c9657e981f6%2Fgettyimages-2297178413.jpg" class="img" alt="Medicare chief Dr. Mehmet Oz is shown standing in front of a huge screen displaying the words, &amp;quot;We need to talk ...&amp;quot;" loading="lazy" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;             &lt;p&gt;                 Medicare chief Dr. Mehmet Oz speaks at an event announcing the artificial intelligence website, America.gov  in Washington, D.C., last month.                 &lt;b class="credit" aria-label="Image credit"&gt;                                          Jim Watson/AFP via Getty Images                                      &lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;b class="hide-caption"&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/p&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;               &lt;button class="toggle-caption" aria-label=""&gt;               &lt;svg class="icon icon--info"&gt;&lt;use href="#icon-info"&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;               &lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;abbr title="Toggle Caption" class="toggle-caption__info"&gt;i&lt;/abbr&gt;              &lt;/button&gt;     &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;p&gt;Medicare's open enrollment window runs through Dec. 7. More than half of Americans in Medicare choose a Medicare Advantage plan, which is run by private insurers instead of the federal government. Medicare Advantage is popular because it covers dental and vision, often with lower premiums than traditional Medicare. But private carriers can pick and choose where it's offered, and withdraw from unprofitable communities. Over the last three years, insurers have left regions on an increasingly large scale, according to recent JAMA research. Here are some &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/10/24/nx-s1-6014612/medicare-advantage-open-enrollment-cancelled-plan" target="_blank"&gt;tips to help navigate&lt;/a&gt; Medicare open enrollment this year:&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;ul class="edTag"&gt;&lt;li&gt;➡️ Check if your Medicare Advantage plan is still offered. Plans closing should have sent letters in early October. You can also log in to Medicare.gov to verify your plan's status.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;➡️ Try calling your state's State Health Insurance Assistance Program. It's free, federally mandated and offers unbiased information on all options. Visit shiphelp.org to find your state's program.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;➡️ Look for local Medicare counseling services. Brokers can be helpful, but make sure to choose one who discusses all your options, including traditional Medicare and supplemental plans.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;➡️ Double-check anything you see on Medicare's Plan Finder with your doctor's office or other Medicare assister.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;➡️ Be cautious about sharing your personal information, such as your phone number. Some online agencies may sell it, leading to spam calls and potential scams.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;       &lt;h3 class="edTag"&gt;3 things to know before you go&lt;/h3&gt;   &lt;div id="resg-s1-150527" class="bucketwrap image large"&gt;             &lt;div     class="i