0px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3840x2160+0+0/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fcc%2Fac%2F936c4970443496dbac79e50eabc6%2Fhelpline-07-leadart.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3840x2160+0+0/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fcc%2Fac%2F936c4970443496dbac79e50eabc6%2Fhelpline-07-leadart.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3840x2160+0+0/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fcc%2Fac%2F936c4970443496dbac79e50eabc6%2Fhelpline-07-leadart.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3840x2160+0+0/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fcc%2Fac%2F936c4970443496dbac79e50eabc6%2Fhelpline-07-leadart.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3840x2160+0+0/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fcc%2Fac%2F936c4970443496dbac79e50eabc6%2Fhelpline-07-leadart.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3840x2160+0+0/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fcc%2Fac%2F936c4970443496dbac79e50eabc6%2Fhelpline-07-leadart.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3840x2160+0+0/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fcc%2Fac%2F936c4970443496dbac79e50eabc6%2Fhelpline-07-leadart.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3840x2160+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fcc%2Fac%2F936c4970443496dbac79e50eabc6%2Fhelpline-07-leadart.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3840x2160+0+0/resize/1100/quality/50/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fcc%2Fac%2F936c4970443496dbac79e50eabc6%2Fhelpline-07-leadart.jpg" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3840x2160+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fcc%2Fac%2F936c4970443496dbac79e50eabc6%2Fhelpline-07-leadart.jpg" class="img" alt="HCHL_7_LeadArt" loading="lazy" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;p&gt;Out-of-pocket maternity expenses are difficult to predict. A federal requirement for insurers to provide personalized cost estimates under the No Surprises Act was supposed to take effect in 2022, but federal regulators haven't made it happen despite pressure from Congress. Until the cost of maternity care gets more transparent, here are some &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/10/21/nx-s1-5996849/insurance-cost-baby-pregnancy" target="_blank"&gt;tips to help you budget&lt;/a&gt; for the cost of pregnancy:&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;ul class="edTag rte2-style-ul"&gt;&lt;li&gt;👶 Enroll your baby in your insurance before your deadline. For most employer health plans, that's within 30 days of birth.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;👶 Check what your maximum out-of-pocket expense is. That's the most you'll pay each year for covered, in-network expenses before your insurance pays 100% of the costs.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;👶 Choose in-network providers whenever possible.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;👶 Go over every bill, and don't be afraid to appeal errors.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;👶 Ask your hospital or other providers about financial assistance.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;   &lt;h3 class="edTag"&gt;Deep dive&lt;/h3&gt;   &lt;div id="resg-s1-149911" class="bucketwrap image large"&gt;             &lt;div     class="imagewrap has-source-dimensions"     data-crop-type=""     style="         --source-width: 2000;         --source-height: 1333;     " &gt;         &lt;picture&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2000x1333+0+0/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fcf%2F87%2F99e0849e47e2813d852eb94b5f40%2Fap26282622911128.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2000x1333+0+0/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fcf%2F87%2F99e0849e47e2813d852eb94b5f40%2Fap26282622911128.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2000x1333+0+0/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fcf%2F87%2F99e0849e47e2813d852eb94b5f40%2Fap26282622911128.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2000x1333+0+0/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fcf%2F87%2F99e0849e47e2813d852eb94b5f40%2Fap26282622911128.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2000x1333+0+0/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fcf%2F87%2F99e0849e47e2813d852eb94b5f40%2Fap26282622911128.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2000x1333+0+0/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fcf%2F87%2F99e0849e47e2813d852eb94b5f40%2Fap26282622911128.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2000x1333+0+0/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fcf%2F87%2F99e0849e47e2813d852eb94b5f40%2Fap26282622911128.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2000x1333+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fcf%2F87%2F99e0849e47e2813d852eb94b5f40%2Fap26282622911128.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2000x1333+0+0/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fcf%2F87%2F99e0849e47e2813d852eb94b5f40%2Fap26282622911128.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2000x1333+0+0/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fcf%2F87%2F99e0849e47e2813d852eb94b5f40%2Fap26282622911128.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2000x1333+0+0/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fcf%2F87%2F99e0849e47e2813d852eb94b5f40%2Fap26282622911128.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2000x1333+0+0/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fcf%2F87%2F99e0849e47e2813d852eb94b5f40%2Fap26282622911128.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2000x1333+0+0/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fcf%2F87%2F99e0849e47e2813d852eb94b5f40%2Fap26282622911128.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2000x1333+0+0/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fcf%2F87%2F99e0849e47e2813d852eb94b5f40%2Fap26282622911128.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2000x1333+0+0/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fcf%2F87%2F99e0849e47e2813d852eb94b5f40%2Fap26282622911128.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2000x1333+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fcf%2F87%2F99e0849e47e2813d852eb94b5f40%2Fap26282622911128.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2000x1333+0+0/resize/1100/quality/50/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fcf%2F87%2F99e0849e47e2813d852eb94b5f40%2Fap26282622911128.jpg" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2000x1333+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fcf%2F87%2F99e0849e47e2813d852eb94b5f40%2Fap26282622911128.jpg" class="img" alt="A satellite image provided by the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration (NOAA) shows Hurricane Isaias approaching the Gulf Coast of Alabama and Florida on Friday, Oct. 9 2026." loading="lazy" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;             &lt;p&gt;                 A satellite image provided by the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration (NOAA) shows Hurricane Isaias approaching the Gulf Coast of Alabama and Florida on Friday, Oct. 9 2026.                 &lt;b class="credit" aria-label="Image credit"&gt;                                          National Oceanic and Atmospheric/National Oceanic and Atmospheric                                      &lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;b class="hide-caption"&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/p&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;               &lt;button class="toggle-caption" aria-label=""&gt;               &lt;svg class="icon icon--info"&gt;&lt;use href="#icon-info"&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;               &lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;abbr title="Toggle Caption" class="toggle-caption__info"&gt;i&lt;/abbr&gt;              &lt;/button&gt;     &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;p&gt;When Hurricane Isaias made landfall in Destin, Fla., as a Category 2 storm, Google DeepMind's AI model outperformed the traditional model, correctly predicting it would hit the Florida Panhandle rather than head west toward Louisiana. Hurricane forecasters say they're increasingly relying on AI technology, and that it's &lt;a href="http://npr.org/nx-s1-6012820" target="_blank"&gt;rapidly transforming the field&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;ul class="edTag rte2-style-ul"&gt;&lt;li&gt;🌀 The National Hurricane Center first started incorporating Google DeepMind into its official forecasts last year. By the end of the 2025 Atlantic hurricane season, it was among the best at predicting a storm's strength and path.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;🌀 Traditional physics-based prediction models use real-time atmospheric data like temperature, humidity, pressure and wind shear. AI models rely more on history and patterns from analyzing thousands of past storms.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;🌀 AI models can feel like a black box; meteorologists depend on them but don't always know why their predictions are correct.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;🌀 Humans still interpret the results of these models and translate them into clear guidance people can act on.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;   &lt;h3 class="edTag"&gt;3 things to know before you go&lt;/h3&gt;   &lt;ol class="edTag rte2-style-ol"&gt;&lt;li&gt;The latest edition of NPR's Breakfast Around the World series takes us to the ancient city of Aleppo in northern Syria, where a sweet semolina pudding called mamounieh &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/07/19/nx-s1-5880425/a-sweet-breakfast-of-mamounieh-in-aleppo-invites-stories-from-fellow-diners" target="_blank"&gt;evokes memories both bitter and sweet&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention has reported 4,588 confirmed measles cases across the U.S. this year through Oct. 22 — &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/10/25/nx-s1-6016205/measles-outbreak-pennsylvania" target="_blank"&gt;more than double&lt;/a&gt; the number for all of 2025. Cases are particularly high in Pennsylvania.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;The general fertility rate for U.S. women &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/10/24/nx-s1-6012804/birth-rate-population-cdc" target="_blank"&gt;fell by 1% to a record low&lt;/a&gt; in 2025, according to the CDC. Birth rates declined among women in their 20s but rose among women ages 30 to 45.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ol&gt;       &lt;p&gt;This newsletter was edited by Majd Al-Waheidi.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;/div&gt;</description>
