      <title>Military reinforcements move to Mideast. And, millions of Americans vote early</title>
      <author>Brittney Melton</author>
      <link>https://www.npr.org/2026/10/23/g-s1-149514/up-first-newsletter-iran-war-midterm-voting-trump-bbc</link>
      <comments>https://text.npr.org/2026/10/23/g-s1-149514/up-first-newsletter-iran-war-midterm-voting-trump-bbc</comments>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.npr.org/2026/10/23/g-s1-149514/up-first-newsletter-iran-war-midterm-voting-trump-bbc</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 23 Oct 2026 11:22:25 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div id="storytext" class="storytext storylocation linkLocation"&gt;       &lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Good morning. You're reading the Up First newsletter. &lt;/em&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/newsletter/news" target="_blank"&gt;Subscribe&lt;/a&gt;&lt;em&gt; here to get it delivered to your inbox, and &lt;/em&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/podcasts/510318/up-first/" target="_blank"&gt;listen&lt;/a&gt;&lt;em&gt; to the Up First podcast for all the news you need to start your day.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;h3 class="edTag"&gt;Today's top stories&lt;/h3&gt;   &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Heavier fighting in Iran could resume in the coming weeks &lt;/strong&gt;as military reinforcements arrive and some U.S. personnel leave the region. This shift comes as a person briefed on the U.S. and Israel's war planning told NPR that the countries were preparing plans for a possible attack on Iran after the U.S. midterms. Iran said &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/10/22/nx-s1-6011514/us-israel-iran-war" target="_blank"&gt;it is prepared&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;div id="resg-s1-149630" class="bucketwrap image large"&gt;             &lt;div     class="imagewrap has-source-dimensions"     data-crop-type=""     style="         --source-width: 8192;         --source-height: 4608;     " &gt;         &lt;picture&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8192x4608+0+428/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd5%2Fa2%2Fd527ed754be4912fafeac24c412a%2Fgettyimages-2300727417.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8192x4608+0+428/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd5%2Fa2%2Fd527ed754be4912fafeac24c412a%2Fgettyimages-2300727417.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8192x4608+0+428/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd5%2Fa2%2Fd527ed754be4912fafeac24c412a%2Fgettyimages-2300727417.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8192x4608+0+428/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd5%2Fa2%2Fd527ed754be4912fafeac24c412a%2Fgettyimages-2300727417.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8192x4608+0+428/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd5%2Fa2%2Fd527ed754be4912fafeac24c412a%2Fgettyimages-2300727417.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8192x4608+0+428/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd5%2Fa2%2Fd527ed754be4912fafeac24c412a%2Fgettyimages-2300727417.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8192x4608+0+428/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd5%2Fa2%2Fd527ed754be4912fafeac24c412a%2Fgettyimages-2300727417.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8192x4608+0+428/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd5%2Fa2%2Fd527ed754be4912fafeac24c412a%2Fgettyimages-2300727417.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8192x4608+0+428/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd5%2Fa2%2Fd527ed754be4912fafeac24c412a%2Fgettyimages-2300727417.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8192x4608+0+428/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd5%2Fa2%2Fd527ed754be4912fafeac24c412a%2Fgettyimages-2300727417.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8192x4608+0+428/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd5%2Fa2%2Fd527ed754be4912fafeac24c412a%2Fgettyimages-2300727417.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8192x4608+0+428/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd5%2Fa2%2Fd527ed754be4912fafeac24c412a%2Fgettyimages-2300727417.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8192x4608+0+428/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd5%2Fa2%2Fd527ed754be4912fafeac24c412a%2Fgettyimages-2300727417.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8192x4608+0+428/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd5%2Fa2%2Fd527ed754be4912fafeac24c412a%2Fgettyimages-2300727417.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8192x4608+0+428/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd5%2Fa2%2Fd527ed754be4912fafeac24c412a%2Fgettyimages-2300727417.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8192x4608+0+428/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd5%2Fa2%2Fd527ed754be4912fafeac24c412a%2Fgettyimages-2300727417.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8192x4608+0+428/resize/1100/quality/50/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd5%2Fa2%2Fd527ed754be4912fafeac24c412a%2Fgettyimages-2300727417.jpg" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8192x4608+0+428/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd5%2Fa2%2Fd527ed754be4912fafeac24c412a%2Fgettyimages-2300727417.jpg" class="img" alt="Traffic in Tehran on Wednesday moves past an installation depicting butterflies forming the Iranian national flag." loading="lazy" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;             &lt;p&gt;                 Traffic in Tehran on Wednesday moves past an installation depicting butterflies forming the Iranian national flag.                 &lt;b class="credit" aria-label="Image credit"&gt;                                          Atta Kenare/AFP via Getty Images                                      &lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;b class="hide-caption"&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/p&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;               &lt;button class="toggle-caption" aria-label=""&gt;               &lt;svg class="icon icon--info"&gt;&lt;use href="#icon-info"&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;               &lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;abbr title="Toggle Caption" class="toggle-caption__info"&gt;i&lt;/abbr&gt;              &lt;/button&gt;     &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;ul class="edTag rte2-style-ul"&gt;&lt;li&gt;🎧 &lt;strong&gt;There are clear signs of preparations for a possible attack&lt;/strong&gt;, NPR's Greg Myre tells &lt;em&gt;Up First&lt;/em&gt;. The U.S. is deploying a third aircraft carrier to the Middle East, and Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth recently visited Israel to meet with Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu. President Trump will likely consider military action after Nov. 3 to avoid a spike in oil prices before the midterm elections. In response, Iran's Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi said that Iran is ready for both diplomacy and war. While the U.S. and Israel possess the power to inflict heavy damage, Iran shows &lt;a href="http://one.npr.org/i/nx-s1-6013298:x" target="_blank"&gt;no signs of making significant concessions&lt;/a&gt;, Myre says. Instead, it demands formal control over the Strait of Hormuz and the withdrawal of U.S. troops from the region.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;       &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Although Election Day is more than a week away&lt;/strong&gt;, almost 21 million Americans have already voted, either by mail or early in-person voting, according to the University of Florida's Election Lab.&lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;ul class="edTag rte2-style-ul"&gt;&lt;li&gt;🎧&lt;strong&gt; That number of votes so far is a lot &lt;/strong&gt;and is an increase of about 2 million since yesterday, according to NPR's Saige Miller. In key battleground states, &lt;a href="http://one.npr.org/i/nx-s1-6013298:x" target="_blank"&gt;early voting is significantly higher&lt;/a&gt; compared to the last midterm election. While the early voting data should not be overinterpreted as a predictor of final results, it shows Democrats voting at higher rates than Republicans, Miller says. Unaffiliated voters are also participating early. Democrats are generally more likely to vote early, and Republicans are more likely to turn out on Election Day, Miller says.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;➡️ &lt;strong&gt;A federal judge in California is limiting the Department of Homeland Security's pre-election field operations. &lt;/strong&gt;The agency says they aim to identify noncitizens who &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/10/22/nx-s1-6012682/judge-dhs-noncitizen-voting-operations" target="_blank"&gt;allegedly voted illegally in nine states&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;➡️&lt;strong&gt; The NPR App has all of NPR's election coverage in one place&lt;/strong&gt; — from the biggest stories across the country, to the issues that matter close to home. Browse headlines, playlists, podcast episodes, videos and more, all on the &lt;a href="https://npr.app/stqe/election-2026" target="_blank"&gt;2026 Election tab&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;   &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Trump must sit for a deposition in his $10 billion defamation case &lt;/strong&gt;against the BBC, a federal magistrate judge ruled. Trump is required to comply with court rules or face sanctions, the judge said yesterday. The case centers on a &lt;em&gt;Panorama&lt;/em&gt; broadcast from late October 2024, which allegedly edited the president's Jan. 6 speech to make it appear he incited a violent siege of Congress. Trump has described the broadcast as false, misleading and defamatory under Florida law.&lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt;       &lt;aside id="mid-story-recommendations-mount-piano" class="mid-story-recommendations-mount-piano" aria-label="recommended stories"&gt;&lt;/aside&gt;    &lt;!-- END ID="MID-STORY-RECOMMENDATIONS-MOUNT-PIANO" CLASS="MID-STORY-RECOMMENDATIONS-MOUNT-PIANO" ARIA-LABEL="RECOMMENDED STORIES" --&gt;    &lt;ul class="edTag rte2-style-ul"&gt;&lt;li&gt;🎧&lt;strong&gt; It's unclear if Trump will sit for a deposition after he previously canceled&lt;/strong&gt; for "matters of national importance" and suggested January as an alternative, NPR's David Folkenflik says. The case initially focused on the economic harm the broadcast posed to Trump's brand and financial prospects. Although the BBC apologized for the splicing, it now &lt;a href="http://one.npr.org/i/nx-s1-6013298:x" target="_blank"&gt;seeks to understand Trump's mindset&lt;/a&gt; during his testimony, as he may have intended to encourage violence. BBC attorneys say more than 200 defendants from Jan. 6 said Trump's speech motivated them. The BBC is actively pursuing details about how Trump has profited since regaining the White House, most likely to assess the extent of the damage he faces, Folkenflik says.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;   &lt;h3 class="edTag"&gt;Deep dive&lt;/h3&gt;   &lt;div id="resg-s1-148836" class="bucketwrap image large"&gt;             &lt;div     class="imagewrap has-source-dimensions"     data-crop-type=""     style="         --source-width: 1990;         --source-height: 1119;     " &gt;         &lt;picture&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/1990x1119+70+8